      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\n- Scheme: A\n- Member state: Slovakia (SLOVAKIA)\n- Date issued: 6th November 2025\n- Page total: 10\n- Project number: maybe \"2025/ER010/SK1\"? That's the type evaluation report number. Actually the certificate number format \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" - project number might not be present. Hmm, could be \"20.02\"? I'll omit project_number since it's not explicit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\" appears in cert number and report number. The OIML Issuing Authority ID is likely \"SK1\".\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\n- Address lines: Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia; Product Certification Body, Hviedzoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\n- Person responsible: Dušan Šmigura, Director of PCB\n\n**Applicant:**\n- Ningbo Aimei Meter Manufacture Co., Ltd., 68, West Town Road, Shangtian Town, Fenghua, Zhejiang, China 3155 (note: likely 315500 but as written \"3155\")\n\n**Manufacturer:**\n- Same as applicant.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: [\"UWM\"]\n- module_designation: The \"Designation of the module\" section says \"Ultrasonic water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water\" — this seems more like a description. Actually the schema says module_designation is \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Here the module designation is given as a description of the meter. I'll use the text? Hmm. The header says \"Designation of the module (if applicable)\" and content is \"Ultrasonic water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water\". This reads more as a description of the certified type. I could put module_designation as that string or as description. I'll put it in description and maybe module_designation... For water meters, there isn't a module per se. I'll set module_designation to null and put the text in description. Actually let me keep description from section 1: \"The ultrasonic water meter type UWM is designed to measure, memorise and display the volume of water passing through the measurement transducer at metering conditions...\" Maybe a short description. I'll use the module designation text as description and maybe include some of section 1.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [2]\n\n**Test reports:**\n- id: \"2025/ER010/SK1\", date: 2025-11-06, pages: 19, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- Documentation file: \"Ningbo Aimei_UWM_01\" dated 6th November 2025, 76 pages, role: \"documentation_file\"\n\n**Revision history:**\n- revision \"0\", date \"17rd July 2020\" (as written — \"17rd\" is odd, keep as written? The schema says revision string, date. I'll normalize date to \"2020-07-17\"? The rules don't specify date format. I'll keep original strings perhaps ISO format. Let me use ISO: 2020-07-17 and 2025-11-06. Changes as described.\n\n**Models / model family:**\nThe cert covers DN15/DN20, Q3 = 1.6/2.5/4, software versions 13 with checksums 21574/25667/22681. There are 8 tables (3.1–3.8):\n\nTable 3.1: DN15, Q3=1.6, checksum 13 25667 (software 13, checksum 25667), Q1/R: R values 125,160,200,250,315,400,500 (7 values), Q1: 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4, 5.08, 4, 3.2 l/h; Q2: 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24, 8.13, 6.4, 5.12; Q4=2 m³/h; Q2/Q1=1.6; pressure loss 0.40 bar Δp40; battery 2 or 3 pcs.\n\nTable 3.2: DN15, Q3=2.5, checksum 25667: R 160–800 (8 values), Q1: 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25, 5, 3.97, 3.125; Q2: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5; Q4=3.125; Δp40; battery 2/3.\n\nTable 3.3: DN20, Q3=2.5, checksum 25667: R 125–500 (7), Q1: 20, 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25, 5; Q2: 32, 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8; Q4=3.125; Δp40; battery 2/3; thread G1 B.\n\nTable 3.4: DN20, Q3=4, checksum 25667: R 160–800 (8), Q1: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10, 8, 6.35, 5; Q2: 40, 32, 25.6, 20.32, 16, 12.8, 10.16, 8; Q4=5; Δp40; battery 2/3.\n\nTable 3.5: DN15, Q3=1.6, checksum 21574: R 125,160,200,250 (4), Q1: 12.8, 10, 8, 6.4; Q2: 20.48, 16, 12.8, 10.24; Q4=2; Δp63; battery 1 pc, 3V–3.6V, 10 years.\n\nTable 3.6: DN15, Q3=2.5, checksum 21574: R 160–400 (5), Q1: 15.625, 12.5, 10, 7.94, 6.25; Q2: 25, 20, 16, 12.7, 10; Q4=3.125; Δp63; battery 1 pc.\n\nTable 3.7: DN20, Q3=2.5, checksum 22681: R 125–250 (4), Q1: 20, 15.625, 12.5, 10; Q2: 32, 25, 20, 16; Q4=3.125; Δp63; battery 1 pc; thread G1 B.\n\nTable 3.8: DN20, Q3=4, checksum 22681: R 200,250,315,400 (4), Q1: 20, 16, 12.7, 10; Q2: 32, 25.6, 20.32, 16; Q4=5; Δp63; battery 1 pc.\n\nSoftware table 2.2:\n- V1.3, DN15: checksums 21574 AND 25667 (two rows)\n- V1.3, DN20: checksums 22681 AND 25667\n\nSo per table heading: \"software and checksum 13 25667\" — software 13 (displayed as V1.3 / SW 13) with checksum 25667, etc.\n\nSo the model variants are combinations of: DN (15/20), Q3 (1.6/2.5/4), checksum (21574/25667/22681). Actually each of the 8 tables is a distinct model configuration. Model ids like \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 (checksum 25667)\".\n\nHow to represent? The model family: family_name \"UWM\". Models: 8 variants keyed by DN/Q3/checksum. Actually the R-values (ratio Q3/Q1) is a further variation axis within each table — each column corresponds to a different R ratio (a meter's Q1 depends on R). So each model (DN, Q3, checksum) is available with multiple R ratios, each giving different Q1/Q2.\n\nThis is a config axis: \"ratio_q3_q1\". So I can use config_level entries with axis \"ratio_q3_q1\" per model... The config_level structure is: attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]. But conditions need to combine model + R ratio. Hmm.\n\nSimplest robust approach:\n- model_family.models: 8 entries with model_id like \"DN15-Q3_1.6-CK25667\" and attributes {nominal_dn: 15, permanent_flowrate_q3: 1.6, checksum: 25667, battery: ...}.\n- matrix_tables: 8 tables preserving full data including ratio columns.\n- model_level characteristics: permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, min_flowrate... but min flowrate varies within model by R. So min/max flowrate per model isn't single-valued; they vary by R ratio.\n\nBetter: for each table, the model_level has ratio_q3_q1 with values per model being a list? Not allowed. Alternative: treat each (DN, Q3, checksum, R) as a separate config. Could model_id include the R? That gives 8 tables × 4–8 R values = 47 variants. That's heavy but accurate.\n\nI think a pragmatic approach:\n- model_family.models: 8 variants (DN × Q3 × checksum).\n- matrix_tables: 8 tables capturing all rows including R and Q1/Q2 values per R.\n- model_level: attributes that are constant per model: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, pressure_loss, battery, material of body, connection thread.\n- config_level: attributes varying by ratio axis: min_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, ratio_q2_q1 (1.6 constant), with axis \"ratio_q3_q1\" and conditions like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 checksum=25667 R=125\" etc.\n\nHmm, config_level values: {condition, value}. The condition would encode model+R. That works.\n\nActually maybe simpler to make model_level entries for q1 and q2 with values where value is a list? No — structured value shape doesn't allow lists.\n\nAlternatively, since the R ratio itself is the selection axis and each R corresponds to a Q1/Q2 pair, and this pattern is identical across checksum variants (e.g., DN15 Q3=1.6 R125: Q1=12.8, Q2=20.48 in both checksum 21574 and 25667 tables), I could define models as (DN, Q3, checksum) and use config_level with axis \"ratio_q3_q1\" and conditions like \"model=DN15 Q3=1.6 checksum=25667; R=125\".\n\nLet me define config_level entries:\n\nattribute: \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis: \"ratio_q3_q1 (R)\", values: [{condition: \"DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, checksum 25667, R=125\", value: 12.8, unit_symbol: \"l/h\"}, ...]. That's 47 entries. Long but faithful.\n\nActually, that's a LOT of JSON. Let me count: Table 3.1: 7; 3.2: 8; 3.3: 7; 3.4: 8; 3.5: 4; 3.6: 5; 3.7: 4; 3.8: 4 → 47 combos. For q1 and q2 both: 94 value entries. Plus matrix_tables rows: 47 rows across 8 tables. This is verbose but the task emphasizes preserving multi-dim tables and matrix detection. I think matrix_tables is the right home for the R-dependent Q1/Q2 data, and I can keep config_level lighter or skip config_level in favor of matrix_tables. Rule 4 R137 example shows matrix_tables AND model_level for model-varying attributes. Here the variation is by model AND by R. The Q1/Q2 data lives naturally in matrix_tables rows. I'll populate:\n\n- matrix_tables: 8 tables, each with columns [nominal_diameter, material_of_body, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1, connection_thread, construction_length, pressure_loss, battery], and rows — one row per R value. That preserves everything.\n\nActually the tables have Q1/Q2 in separate rows spanning columns matching R values. A row per R: {dn: 15, q3: 1.6, ratio_q3_q1: 125, q1: 12.8, q2: 20.48, ...}. Common attributes repeated per row or factored into table description. I'll include per-row: nominal_diameter, q3, checksum (in table name/description), ratio_q3_q1, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2. Table-level: overload q4, ratio_q2_q1, connection thread, length, pressure loss, battery. Hmm, matrix_tables rows schema says \"object per row\" — freeform. I'll do rows with q1/q2/ratio and put the constant stuff in the table \"description\"? The schema has \"description\" and \"footnotes\". I could include constant attributes in each row to be safe. Let me include: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, ratio_q3_q1, minimum_flowrate_q1 (l/h), transitional_flowrate_q2 (l/h). And overload_q4 etc. repeated too — fine, just repeat them; it's data fidelity.\n\nActually, to keep it manageable, rows: {\"ratio_q3_q1\": 125, \"minimum_flowrate_q1\": 12.8, \"transitional_flowrate_q2\": 20.48} and put dn/q3/checksum in name + description. Columns: [\"ratio_q3_q1\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1_l_h\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2_l_h\"]. Hmm the schema example used column_units separate. I'll include column_units.\n\nWait, the schema for matrix_tables: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]. The R137 example also shows column_units even though not in the yaml schema — the example includes it. I'll include column_units as it's demonstrated in the example.\n\n- model_level: for the 8 models: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, pressure_loss (bar), battery_count, battery_voltage_max, battery_life, software_checksum... Some of these differ per model (checksum, q4, pressure loss, battery).\n\nLet me structure models with attributes directly on the model entries, and also model_level for key varying attributes. The schema model entry has \"attributes{}\". So I can put per-model attributes in the models[] entries. Then model_level for the matrix-style attributes like q3, q4, checksum.\n\nModel IDs: I'll use like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_ck25667\"? Or cleaner: \"UWM DN15 Q3 1.6 (13 25667)\". Let me use model_id: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-25667\" and label \"UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, software 13 checksum 25667\". Attributes: {nominal_diameter: 15, permanent_flowrate_q3: 1.6, software_checksum: \"25667\", ...}.\n\nHmm wait — for DN15 Q3=1.6, checksum 25667 and 21574 both exist (tables 3.1 and 3.5). DN15 Q3=2.5: 25667 and 21574 (3.2, 3.6). DN20 Q3=2.5: 25667 and 22681 (3.3, 3.7). DN20 Q3=4: 25667 and 22681 (3.4, 3.8). So 8 models.\n\nAlso software table 2.2 says DN15 has checksums 21574 and 25667; DN20 has 22681 and 25667 — consistent.\n\nBattery: tables with 25667 → 2 or 3 pcs, Umin 2.7V Umax 3.7V, life up to 16 years. Tables 21574/22681 → 1 pc, Umin 3V Umax 3.6V, life up to 10 years. Interesting — battery depends on checksum. Wait, table 3.1 (25667): \"2 or 3 pcs Umin 2,7V, Umax 3,7V, life time up to 16 years\". Table 3.5 (21574): \"1 pc li-battery Umin 3V, Umax 3,6V, life time up to 10 years\". Yes.\n\nPressure loss: 25667 tables → Δp40, 0.40 bar. 21574/22681 tables → Δp63, 0.63 bar.\n\nSo checksum variants differ in battery and pressure loss class. Good.\n\nTemperature class (from 3.9): T30, T50 brass and plastic body; T70 brass body. Water temp: 0.1–30 °C, 0.1–50 °C, 0.1–70 °C accordingly.\n\n**Type-level characteristics (3.9):**\n- installation_orientation: \"All positions\"\n- water_temperature_range: 0.1–30, 0.1–50, 0.1–70 °C depending on temp class/body — this is a config axis (body material / temperature class). config_level: axis \"temperature_class / body material\".\n- temperature_class: T30, T50 (brass and plastic), T70 (brass) — config axis.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- maximum_permissible_error_upper_range: ±2% (T ≤ 30°C), ±3% (T > 30°C) — config_level.\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_range: ±5%\n- capacity_of_calculator: 99999.99999 m³\n- scale_interval: 0.00001 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_class: M1\n- climatic_class: -25 to +55 °C\n- electromagnetic_class: E1 (brass), E1/E2 (plastic) — config axis.\n- environmental conditions class: B/O\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: U0 D0\n\nFor type_level with single values: max admissible pressure 16 bar, MPE lower ±5%, capacity, scale interval, accuracy class 2, mechanical class M1, climatic class -25..+55 °C, flow profile U0D0, environmental class B/O, installation orientation all positions.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- temperature_class: axis \"body_material\", values: [{condition \"brass body\", value \"T30, T50, T70\"}, {condition \"plastic body\", value \"T30, T50\"}]. Hmm, from 3.9: \"T30, T50 brass and plastic body; T70 brass body\".\n- water_temperature_range: axis \"temperature_class\": T30: 0.1–30, T50: 0.1–50, T70: 0.1–70 °C.\n- electromagnetic_class: axis \"body_material\": brass: E1; plastic: E1, E2.\n- maximum_permissible_error_upper_range: axis \"water_temperature\": ≤30°C: ±2; >30°C: ±3.\n\nValues for MPE: ±2 → value: {min: -2, max: 2}? Or string \"±2\"? I'd use value: 2 with a note... Schema: value number|range|string. \"± 2\" — I'll use {min: -2, max: 2}, unit \"%\". Hmm, that's an error limit ±2%. Range -2 to 2 is reasonable. Or keep string \"±2\". I'll use range {min:-2, max:2}. Actually for maximum permissible error, typically expressed as ±2%. I think {min: -2, max: 2} is fine and numeric.\n\nFor q1/q2 values in config_level: I decided to keep them in matrix_tables. But maybe also provide config_level for ratio_q3_q1 per model? The R values per model: this is genuinely a model attribute (the meter is marked \"Q3 2.5 R=250\"). Hmm. Each table lists R options. I could add model_level entry \"ratio_q3_q1_options\" — but values are multiple per model. Not allowed.\n\nAlternative: treat each (DN, Q3, checksum, R) combination as a model variant in model_family.models. 47 models. Each with attributes: dn, q3, q4, checksum, R, q1, q2, pressure loss, battery. That's very complete but big. Hmm. 47 model objects × ~8 attributes. That's heavy but thorough. The guidance says \"one entry per variant\". The variants differ by R (different Q1 markings). But the tables treat R as a range selection, not distinct models. Typically the model is \"UWM\" and ordering covers all combos.\n\nI think a middle ground: models = 8 (dn/q3/checksum), with attributes including \"available_ratios_q3_q1\": [125,160,...] as a list attribute (allowed? attributes{} is freeform — yes, attributes values are freeform JSON). Then matrix_tables carry the full R×Q1/Q2 grid. And model_level: permanent_flowrate_q3 (per model), overload_flowrate_q4 (per model), minimum_flowrate... no, keep q1/q2 in matrix tables only. Hmm, but rule 4 says populate characteristics.model_level too for matrix tables. The varying-per-model attributes: nominal_diameter, q3, q4, checksum, pressure_loss, battery. Let me create model_level entries for: nominal_diameter (mm), permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h), overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h), software_checksum (dimensionless string), pressure_loss (bar), pressure_loss_class, battery_quantity, battery_voltage_max (V), battery_life_years.\n\nModel ids: let me define:\n1. \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 ck25667\" — hmm, cleaner: \"DN15_Q3_1.6_ck25667\".\n\nI'll use model_id like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 (checksum 25667)\". Label same-ish.\n\nLet me enumerate models with attributes:\n\nM1: DN15, Q3=1.6, ck=25667, Q4=2 m³/h, R options 125–500 (7), Δp40 (0.40 bar), battery 2-3 pcs Umax 3.7V 16y, thread G ¾ B / G 7/8 B-G ¾ B\nM2: DN15, Q3=2.5, ck=25667, Q4=3.125, R 160–800 (8), Δp40, battery 2-3, thread G ¾ B / G 7/8 B-G ¾ B\nM3: DN20, Q3=2.5, ck=25667, Q4=3.125, R 125–500 (7), Δp40, battery 2-3, thread G1 B\nM4: DN20, Q3=4, ck=25667, Q4=5, R 160–800 (8), Δp40, battery 2-3, thread G1 B\nM5: DN15, Q3=1.6, ck=21574, Q4=2, R 125–250 (4), Δp63 (0.63 bar), battery 1 pc Umax 3.6V 10y, thread G ¾ B / G 7/8 B-G ¾ B\nM6: DN15, Q3=2.5, ck=21574, Q4=3.125, R 160–400 (5), Δp63, battery 1, thread G ¾ B / G 7/8\nM7: DN20, Q3=2.5, ck=22681, Q4=3.125, R 125–250 (4), Δp63, battery 1, thread G1 B\nM8: DN20, Q3=4, ck=22681, Q4=5, R 200–400 (4), Δp63, battery 1, thread G1 B\n\nConstruction length: 110–190 mm all.\n\nConnection thread: DN15: \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B - G ¾ B\" (two options); DN20: \"G 1 B\". Note table 9 (Fig 2) says A column: DN15: \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B-G3/4 B\"; DN20 \"G1 B\". Also dimensions: DN15: L 110–190, H 87, W 81; DN20: L 110–190, H 90, W 81.\n\nI could add a matrix table for dimensions (Fig 2): columns dn_size, l, h, w, thread. Yes, include as a second matrix table \"Basic dimensions\".\n\nComponents:\n- flow sensor: role \"flow_sensor\", type_designations maybe [] — description: cylindrical brass or plastic body... characteristics: body material options brass OR plastic.\n- calculator and indication device: role \"calculator_and_indication_device\", characteristics: display \"electronic LCD non-permanent (automatic scrolling), 10 digits, indication range 99999.99999 m³\", display duration 10 s.\n- battery: component with options: \"two or three non-replaceable lithium batteries Umax=3.7V, life up to 16 years\" OR \"one non-replaceable lithium battery Umax=3.6V, life up to 10 years\" — alternatives \"OR\".\n- Non-essential: non-return valve (optional), communication interfaces: optical infrared, RF, LoRaWAN, wM-Bus, OMS, Sigfox, NB-IoT, M-Bus, Pulse.\n\nMaybe include components for flow sensor and calculator, and optionally battery. I'll include.\n\nNow footnotes: none present. revision_history as given.\n\nDates: date_issued \"2025-11-06\". Revision 0 date \"17rd July 2020\" → 2020-07-17 (normalize). I'll use ISO format.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". number field: full string? The schema: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll set number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\", revision: \"Rev. 1\"? Schema has no revision field at cert level... there's revision_history. I'll include number as \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\"? Hmm. Maybe number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" and rely on revision_history for Rev 1. But then two certs (Rev 0 and Rev 1) share number... Actually Rev 0 was numbered differently (\"formal the year 2020 to 20 in the Certificate number\"). The current certificate number includes \"Rev. 1\". I'll keep the full \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" as number to be faithful. Hmm, but canonical would be without. I'll include \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\"? Let me think: certificate.number should be the certificate identifier. The document repeatedly says OIML Certificate No. R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1. I'll use that full string.\n\nproject_number: not present explicitly. Omit.\n\noiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\" (appears in cert number \"-SK1-\" and report \"2025/ER010/SK1\"). Include at both cert and issuing_authority level.\n\npage_total: 10.\n\nmember_state: \"Slovakia\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2025-11-06\" (signature date 6th November 2025; revision 1 date same).\n\nRecommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, amendment: null, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]} — accuracy_classes as numbers? \"For accuracy class (if applicable): 2\". Water meter accuracy class 2 (metrological). I'll use [2] or [\"2\"]. Schema says accuracy_classes[] — I'll use [2]? For R49 class 2. Hmm strings vs numbers — I'll use [\"2\"]? Example doesn't specify. I'll use [2] numeric... Actually class identifiers are usually strings like \"C\", \"2\". I'll go with [\"2\"]. Hmm, risk either way; choose [\"2\"]? Let me use [2]. The value 2 is numeric in tables. Fine: [2].\n\nSoftware specification table: matrix table? It's a small table: software version V1.3, size DN15/DN20, checksums. Could include as matrix_tables entry \"Software versions and checksums\". Yes, add.\n\nChecksum display note: \"13\" is displayed software version (V1.3 shown as 13). Software version: \"V1.3\" (displayed as \"13\" / \"SW 13\").\n\nNow units: q1, q2 in l/h; q3, q4 in m³/h; pressure loss bar; MAP bar; capacity m³; scale interval m³; climatic °C; lengths mm; battery V.\n\nDecimals: convert commas: 1,6 → 1.6; 12,8 → 12.8; 20,48 → 20.48; 10,24 → 10.24; 8,13 → 8.13; 5,08 → 5.08; 15,625 → 15.625; 7,94 → 7.94; 3,97 → 3.97; 3,125 → 3.125; 12,7 → 12.7; 6,35 → 6.35; 25,6 → 25.6; 20,32 → 20.32; 10,16 → 10.16; 0,40 → 0.40 (0.4); 0,63 → 0.63; 99999,99999 → 99999.99999; 0,00001 → 0.00001; -25 to +55; 3,7 V → 3.7; 3,6 V → 3.6; 2,7 V → 2.7.\n\nNow let's write model_level entries. Each values[] with 8 model refs. Attributes:\n\n1. nominal_diameter: mm, values: DN15 → 15 (M1,M2,M5,M6), DN20 → 20 (M3,M4,M7,M8).\n2. permanent_flowrate_q3: m³/h: M1 1.6, M2 2.5, M3 2.5, M4 4, M5 1.6, M6 2.5, M7 2.5, M8 4.\n3. overload_flowrate_q4: m³/h: M1 2, M2 3.125, M3 3.125, M4 5, M5 2, M6 3.125, M7 3.125, M8 5.\n4. ratio_q3_q1_options: hmm, list per model. attributes{} on models can hold \"ratios_q3_q1\": [..]. For model_level, attribute must have single value per model. A list of R values as value? The value shape is number|range|string — no lists.