15\", construction_length_l: {110,190}, height_h: 87, width_w: 81, connection_thread: \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B-G3/4 B\"}, {dn_size: \"DN20\", l: 110–190, h: 90, w: 81, thread: \"G1 B\"}. Units mm.\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- installation_orientation: \"All positions\"\n- temperature_class: config (body material) — I'll do config_level.\n- water_temperature_range: config (temperature class).\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- maximum_permissible_error_upper_range: config (water temperature).\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_range: {min:-5,max:5} % — type_level.\n- capacity_of_calculator: 99999.99999 m³\n- scale_interval: 0.00001 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-25,max:55} °C\n- electromagnetic_class: config (body material).\n- environmental_class: \"B / O\" — description \"fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors\". attribute \"climatic_and_mechanical_environmental_class\": value \"B / O\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\"\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- material_of_body: \"brass / plastic\" — maybe type_level string value \"brass / plastic\".\n- construction_length: {min:110, max:190} mm\n- software_version: \"V1.3\"\n- display_digits: 10\n- display indication range = capacity.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. attribute temperature_class, axis \"body_material\", values: [{condition \"brass\", value: \"T30, T50, T70\"}, {condition \"plastic\", value: \"T30, T50\"}].\n2. attribute water_temperature_range, axis \"temperature_class\", values: [{condition \"T30\", value {min:0.1,max:30}, unit \"°C\"}, {condition \"T50\", value {min:0.1,max:50}}, {condition \"T70\", value {min:0.1,max:70}}].\n3. attribute electromagnetic_class, axis \"body_material\", values: [{brass: \"E1\"}, {plastic: \"E1, E2\"}].\n4. attribute maximum_permissible_error_upper_range, axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition \"≤ 30 °C\", value {min:-2,max:2}, unit \"%\"}, {condition \"> 30 °C\", value {min:-3,max:3}, unit \"%\"}].\n\nMPE ± values: I'll use {min:-2, max:2}. Reasonable.\n\nComponents:\n[\n {role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [], alternatives: null, characteristics: {...}} — schema components: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll write description-ish attributes in characteristics: {\"body_material\": {\"value\": \"brass OR plastic\"}...}. Keep simple: characteristics: {\"body\": \"cylindrical brass or plastic body with inlet and outlet threaded connections\", \"measuring_principle\": \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\"}. Hmm characteristics should be structured values... it's components characteristics{} freeform. I'll use simple strings.\n\nActually let me structure: \n- flow_sensor: characteristics {\"body_material\": \"brass or plastic\", \"connections\": \"inlet and outlet threaded\", \"measuring_principle\": \"ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time\"}\n- calculator_and_indication_device: characteristics {\"display\": \"electronic LCD non-permanent (automatic scrolling)\", \"digits\": 10, \"indication_range_m3\": 99999.99999, \"display_duration_s\": 10}\n- battery (alternatives OR): two entries? alternatives \"OR\" between battery variants: \"two or three non-replaceable lithium batteries, Umax=3.7V, life up to 16 years\" OR \"one non-replaceable lithium battery, Umax=3.6V, life up to 10 years\". Represent as one component with alternatives: \"OR\" and characteristics {\"option_1\": \"...\", \"option_2\": \"...\"}? Or two components each role \"battery\" with a note. Schema: components[] each with role, type_designations, alternatives, characteristics. I'll create one component role \"battery\" alternatives \"OR\" with characteristics listing options structured: {\"options\": [...]}. Freeform ok.\n\nOptional: non-return valve and communication interfaces — I can note as component \"optional_equipment\". I'll add role \"non_return_valve\" optional and role \"communication_interfaces\" with list. Maybe include as one component \"optional parts\". Let me include two entries: {role: \"non_return_valve\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {\"optional\": true}}, {role: \"communication_interface\", characteristics: {\"available_types\": [\"optical infrared\",\"RF\",\"LoRaWAN\",\"wM-Bus\",\"OMS\",\"Sigfox\",\"NB-IoT\",\"M-Bus\",\"Pulse\"]}}.\n\nApplicant address: \"68, West Town Road, Shangtian Town, Fenghua\", \"Zhejiang, China 3155\". Keep as given (\"3155\" — likely OCR of 315500, but don't fabricate; keep \"3155\").\n\nIssuing authority address_lines: [\"Geologická 9966/1\", \"821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia\", \"Product Certification Body\", \"Hviedzoslavova 31\", \"974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\"]. person_responsible: \"Dušan Šmigura\", person_title: \"Director of PCB\".\n\nDescription (certified_type.description): combine: \"The ultrasonic water meter type UWM is a compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device designed to measure, memorise and display the volume of water passing through the measurement transducer at metering conditions. The measurement is based on ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle. Intended for measurement of volume of clean water in residential, commercial and light industrial use; can be installed in all positions; not designed to measure reverse flow.\"\n\nmodule_designation: The field in the doc: \"Ultrasonic water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water\". Given the schema semantics (\"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null), the R49 module designation is this string. I'll set it to that string since the certificate literally has \"Designation of the module\". Hmm — it might confuse. But rule 8 says extract what's present. I'll set module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water\"? Actually, I worry: in R46/R49 certs, \"designation of the module\" would be like \"heat source\" etc. Here it's a description of the meter itself. I'll include it as given — it's what the document states under that heading.\n\nHmm, alternatively put that in description and module_designation null. The heading explicitly says \"Designation of the module (if applicable): Ultrasonic water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water\". I'll include it verbatim in module_designation. And description gets the section-1 text.\n\ncategory: \"Water meter\".\n\ntype_designations: [\"UWM\"]. Also \"UWM-Cr3\" appears in a photo label — that's a marking on the meter photo (\"Ultrasonic Water Meter Model UWM-Cr3\"). Should I include? It might be a real model designation. It appears only in the figure OCR text. I'll include \"UWM-Cr3\"? Risky—it could be a stale photo. The photo shows \"Model UWM-Cr3\" text. Hmm. I'll include both in type_designations? The certified type is UWM. The figure shows UWM-Cr3 as model text. I think safer to include only \"UWM\". Actually including UWM-Cr3 as a mentioned model identifier could be legit (\"all type/model identifiers mentioned\"). But it may be an old marking on a sample. I'll include just \"UWM\" to be safe.\n\nRecommendation accuracy_classes: [2]? Wait — the certificate says \"For accuracy class (if applicable): 2\". R49 uses accuracy classes 1 and 2. So [\"2\"]. I'll write [2]... Let me decide: JSON numbers vs strings. Schema example: accuracy_classes[] unspecified. I'll use [\"2\"]? For load cells classes like \"C3\" strings make sense. \"2\" as string keeps uniformity. I'll use [\"2\"].\n\nHmm, in the characteristics type_level accuracy_class: value 2 (number). Fine.\n\nScheme: \"A\".\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"2025/ER010/SK1\", date: \"2025-11-06\", pages: 19, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"Ningbo Aimei_UWM_01\", date: \"2025-11-06\", pages: 76, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2020-07-17\", changes: \"Certificate first issued\"}\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2025-11-06\", changes: \"Add: Ratio to R800; temperature class T70 for brass body; checksum 25667; options of trademarks (6 pcs); plastic body for DN15 and DN20 with temperature class T30 and T50. Changes: one pc battery to 2 or 3 pcs batteries; address of OIML Issuing Authority SLM; formal the year \"2020\" to \"20\" in the Certificate number\"}\n\nfootnotes: [] (none). Omit.\n\nDate format: I'll use ISO. \n\nNow, model_id naming. Let me finalize:\n- \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 ck25667\"? I'll use readable: model_id: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-ck25667\". Hmm \"ck\" odd. Use \"checksum25667\". Model IDs:\n1. \"UWM-DN15-Q3_1.6-25667\"\nEh. Simpler: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 (13 25667)\" matching cert language \"software and checksum 13 25667\". I'll use model_id \"DN15_Q3=1.6_ck25667\"... Let me just choose: model_id: \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 checksum 25667\". Fine.\n\nModels array (8 entries) with attributes: nominal_diameter (mm), permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h), software_version (\"V1.3\"/\"13\"), software_checksum, overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h), ratio_q3_q1 options (list), pressure_loss (bar + class), battery.\n\nI'll include in attributes (structured values):\n{\"nominal_diameter\": {\"value\": 15, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}, \"permanent_flowrate_q3\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"}, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"}, \"software_checksum\": {\"value\": \"25667\"}, \"ratio_q3_q1_options\": {\"value\": [125,160,200,250,315,400,500]}} — list inside value breaks shape. Attributes{} is freeform; rule 2 applies to characteristics. For ratio options I'll use {\"value\": {\"min\": 125, \"max\": 500}}. OK.\n\nmodel_level entries: attributes: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1 (range), software_checksum, pressure_loss, pressure_loss_class, battery_quantity, battery_voltage_min, battery_voltage_max, battery_life, connection_thread.\n\nEach values[] has 8 entries (one per model). That's 12 × 8 = 96 entries. Acceptable.\n\nModel references — I'll use short ids in values: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 25667\"? Must match model_id exactly. I'll define model_id strings first:\n\nM1: \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 checksum 25667\"\nM2: \"UWM DN15 Q3=2.5 checksum 25667\"\nM3: \"UWM DN20 Q3=2.5 checksum 25667\"\nM4: \"UWM DN20 Q3=4 checksum 25667\"\nM5: \"UWM DN15 Q3=1.6 checksum 21574\"\nM6: \"UWM DN15 Q3=2.5 checksum 21574\"\nM7: \"UWM DN20 Q3=2.5 checksum 22681\"\nM8: \"UWM DN20 Q3=4 checksum 22681\"\n\nThese are long for values[] refs but fine.\n\nAlternatively short: \"DN15/1.6/25667\". I'll go with the descriptive ones; JSON size acceptable.\n\nNow write model_level:\n\n1. nominal_diameter (mm): M1 15, M2 15, M3 20, M4 20, M5 15, M6 15, M7 20, M8 20.\n2. permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4.\n3. overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): 2, 3.125, 3.125, 5, 2, 3.125, 3.125, 5.\n4. ratio_q3_q1 (R): ranges: M1 {125,500}, M2 {160,800}, M3 {125,500}, M4 {160,800}, M5 {125,250}, M6 {160,400}, M7 {125,250}, M8 {200,400}. unit null (ratio R). Maybe unit_symbol \"R\"? The table says unit \"R\". I'll use unit_symbol \"R\".\n5. software_checksum: \"25667\"×4, \"21574\"×2, \"22681\"×2.\n6. pressure_loss (bar): 0.4,0.4,0.4,0.4,0.63,0.63,0.63,0.63.\n7. pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"×4, \"Δp63\"×4.\n8. battery_quantity: {min:2,max:3} ×4; 1 ×4. unit null (pcs).\n9. battery_voltage_min (V): 2.7 ×4; 3 ×4.\n10. battery_voltage_max (V): 3.7 ×4; 3.6 ×4.\n11. battery_life (years): 16 ×4; 10 ×4.\n12. connection_thread: \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B – G ¾ B\" for DN15 (M1,M2,M5,M6); \"G 1 B\" DN20 (M3,M4,M7,M8).\n\nWait: battery voltage min for 1-pc: \"Umin 3V\" → 3. OK.\n\ntype_level additions: material_of_body \"brass / plastic\", construction_length {110,190} mm, software_version \"V1.3\", ratio_q2_q1 1.6, battery_type \"non-replaceable lithium\" (maybe skip), installation_orientation \"All positions\", etc.\n\nAlso display: type_level \"display_digits\": 10, \"indication_range\": 99999.99999 m³ — same as capacity_of_calculator. Include capacity_of_calculator only.\n\nNow config_level for temperature etc. (4 entries as above).\n\nCheck \"Climatic and mechanical environmental conditions (class)\": \"B / O (fixed meters installed in a building / outdoors)\". type_level: climatic_and_mechanical_environmental_class: \"B / O\".\n\nMarks: skip (section 4 list) — not characteristics.\n\nTrademarks: 6 options listed. Could add to type_level: trademarks: [\"AIMEI\",\"ASM\",\"A/S/M ®\",\"A/S/M ® AIMEI\",\"Ⓢ ®\",\"Ⓢ ® AIMEI\"]. Might be nice; include as matrix? I'll add a type_level characteristic \"trademarks\": value list? Value shape excludes lists... I could put into certified_type.description or skip. I'll add to matrix_tables? Overkill. I'll mention in description briefly. Or omit. I'll add \"trademarks\" as a freeform note in description. Hmm, keep description focused. I'll skip trademarks... Actually rule 8: don't fabricate, but including extra info isn't fabricating. I'll add a components-less note: skip. Let me include in certified_type a \"description\" that mentions markings? No—keep it clean; skip trademarks.\n\nOIML issuer id: \"SK1\".\n\nCertificate \"number\": I'll use \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\"? Hmm. The scheme letter A is in the number too. I'll set number to \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02\" and... no wait, every page header says \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\" as the certificate number. The Rev 1 is part of the number as printed. But revision_history captures Rev. I'll go with full string \"R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". Hmm, but automated consumers might prefer without. The doc explicitly: \"OIML Certificate No. R49/2013-A-SK1-20.02 Rev. 1\". Keep full.\n\nproject_number: omit (not present). page_total: 10.\n\nNow matrix tables in detail.\n\nTable 3.1:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Technical and metrological data UWM DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, software 13, checksum 25667\",\n  \"columns\": [\"nominal_diameter\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length\", \"pressure_loss\", \"battery\"],\n  ...\n}\n\nI decided rows minimal: {ratio_q3_q1, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2}; description carries constants. Let me write description: \"Nominal diameter DN 15 mm; material of body brass/plastic; Q3 = 1.6 m³/h; overload flowrate Q4 = 2 m³/h; ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; connection thread G ¾ B or G 7/8 B – G ¾ B; construction length 110–190 mm; pressure loss class Δp40 (0.40 bar); battery 2 or 3 pcs, Umin 2.7 V, Umax 3.7 V, life time up to 16 years.\"\n\nRows for 3.1 (7 rows). All right.\n\nAlso include q4 and others per-row? Minimal is fine given description. But hmm — matrix_tables rows example in R137 includes all columns as values. The guidance: \"preserve multi-dim tables\". I'll do rows with the three varying values and keep constants in description. Good compromise.\n\nActually wait — maybe better to include nominal_diameter and permanent_flowrate_q3 in each row too so rows are self-contained. It's only 2 extra fields × 47 rows = 94 extra fields. It aids downstream use. I'll include dn and q3 per row. And q4? Also constant per table; include for completeness: yes, include overload_flowrate_q4 per row too. And ratio_q2_q1 = 1.6 per row? That's constant globally... I'll include it; cheap. So rows: {nominal_diameter: 15, permanent_flowrate_q3: 1.6, overload_flowrate_q4: 2, ratio_q2_q1: 1.6, ratio_q3_q1: 125, minimum_flowrate_q1: 12.8, transitional_flowrate_q2: 20.48}. Columns: [\"nominal_diameter\",\"permanent_flowrate_q3\",\"overload_flowrate_q4\",\"ratio_q2_q1\",\"ratio_q3_q1\",\"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\"transitional_flowrate_q2\"], column_units: [\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null,null,\"l/h\",\"l/h\"].\n\n47 rows total across 8 tables. OK.\n\nSoftware table:\n{\n \"name\": \"Software versions and checksums\",\n \"columns\": [\"software_version\", \"meter_size\", \"checksum\"],\n \"column_units\": [null, null, null],\n \"rows\": [\n   {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"meter_size\": \"DN 15\", \"checksum\": \"21574\"},\n   {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"meter_size\": \"DN 15\", \"checksum\": \"25667\"},\n   {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"meter_size\": \"DN 20\", \"checksum\": \"22681\"},\n   {\"software_version\": \"V1.3\", \"meter_size\": \"DN 20\", \"checksum\": \"25667\"}\n ],\n \"description\": \"Software version V1.3 is displayed as 13; checksums can be checked on the screen and on the type plate. Remarks: See tables in point 3.\"\n}\n\nDimensions table:\n{\n \"name\": \"Basic dimensions of the water meter UWM\",\n \"columns\": [\"dn_size\", \"construction_length\", \"height\", \"width\", \"connection_thread\"],\n \"column_units\": [null, \"mm\", \"mm\", \"mm\", null],\n \"rows\": [\n   {\"dn_size\": \"DN15\", \"construction_length\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}, \"height\": 87, \"width\": 81, \"connection_thread\": \"G ¾ B, G 7/8 B-G3/4 B\"},\n   {\"dn_size\": \"DN20\", \"construction_length\": {\"min\": 110, \"max\": 190}, \"height\": 90, \"width\": 81, \"connection_thread\": \"G1 B\"}\n ],\n \"description\": \"Fig. 2 basic dimensions; L is selectable from 110 to 190 mm.\"\n}\n\nNow type_level list final:\n- installation_orientation: \"All positions\" (string value)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_range: {min:-5,max:5} %  (value \"±5\"? I'll use range)\n\nHmm, actually for MPE maybe value string \"±5\" is more faithful. The schema: value number | {min,max} | string. For ±5%, I'll use {min:-5,max:5}. Fine.\n\n- capacity_of_calculator: 99999.99999 m³\n- scale_interval: 0.00001 m³\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-25,max:55} °C\n- climatic_and_mechanical_environmental_class: \"B / O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\"\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- material_of_body: \"brass / plastic\"\n- construction_length: {min:110,max:190} mm\n- software_version: \"V1.3\"\n- battery_type: \"non-replaceable lithium\" — include? type_level ok: value string.\n- display_digits: 10?